From f1b81aee0da8f609ab500497ca23925cbb747973 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 08:35:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] Respawn guard: a core-update requeue no longer
 consumes a PR owner's handoff

hermes-sync's core drain requeues every restart-safe worker with
`hermes kanban reclaim --reason 'hermes-sync core update: restart-safe worker
requeue'`. The one-shot PR-owner handoffs (an orderly dependency park, a review
checkpoint via resumes_review, a same-second immediate checkpoint) were consumed
by "the latest run", so that requeued run burned them and the dispatcher then
held the owner `active_pr` with no worker (2026-11-12: t_01a1ce8aff1f09c1 on
worker-5 sat 12 min after its exact-head APPROVE until an operator intervened).

New hermes_cli/kanban_core_update_requeue.py recognises only a manual reclaim
with exactly that reason, on a run that ended `reclaimed` with no crash,
timeout, spawn_failed or gave_up event. _dependency_handoff_after,
_immediate_checkpoint_after and resumes_review now pass over such runs (and
ignore their `reclaimed` event), so the next respawn resumes as if the requeue
never happened. Any other reclaim, crash or timeout still consumes the handoff,
and a PR the requeued run reported is a newer PR comment that re-arms the guard.

resumes_review did not release the incident card for the same reason: it joins
the dependency_wait park to `MAX(task_runs.id)`, which was the requeued run.

Tests: tests/hermes_cli/test_kanban_core_update_requeue_handoff.py (15), RED
before (3 requeue cases fail) and GREEN after; crash/other-reason reclaims stay
held in both.
